{
  "result": "I love early mornings when the world hasn't quite decided to wake up yet. The light comes in sideways through the blinds, pale and gold, and the coffee maker gurgles like an old friend clearing his throat. For a few minutes, nothing is demanded of you. No emails, no headlines, no small urgencies tapping on your shoulder. Just the quiet possibility that today could be different.\n\nI think we underestimate boredom. We fill every crack with podcasts and scrolling and noise, afraid of what might surface in silence. But boredom is fertile. It's the compost heap where strange ideas grow. When I was a kid, summer afternoons stretched forever, and out of sheer restlessness I'd invent games, build forts from couch cushions, write terrible poems about my dog. That restlessness was a gift.\n\nNow, as an adult with too many tabs open, literally and metaphorically, I'm trying to relearn how to wander. To take a walk without a destination or a step count. To read a book slowly, rereading paragraphs just because the sentences are beautiful. To call a friend without a reason. To notice how rain smells different on hot pavement than on grass.\n\nMaybe freedom isn't grand adventures or dramatic escapes. Maybe it's attention. The ability to choose what you linger on. If I could wish one thing for everyone today, it would be ten undistracted minutes to watch the light change, to breathe deeply, and to remember that being alive is already quite interesting every single day.",
